Static spatial-audio implementation tests
=======================================

From the Wine source root, with a configured Wine build available:

  make -C dlls/mmdevapi/native-tests check \
      WINE_BUILD_DIR=/path/to/configured/wine/build \
      BUILD_DIR=/tmp/mmdevapi-spatial-tests

The test includes the actual spatialaudio.c implementation and replaces
allocation, locking, handle operations, and the COM audio endpoint with
controlled native fixtures. No audio server, Wine runtime, or device is used.
Each case runs in a child process so an old implementation's allocation-failure
crash does not prevent the remaining cases from being checked.

These tests exercise error cleanup, concurrent object activation/release, and
update-cycle state. They do not establish Windows conformance, audio quality,
or behavior on real devices.

The `.c.test` suffix keeps the standalone fixture out of Wine's generated
source lists. The native Makefile compiles it explicitly as C.

Endpoint hotplug identity and synthetic-device removal:

    python3 endpoint-identity.py --build-dir /tmp/mmdevapi-identity-tests

This fixture extracts the production identity-comparison, endpoint creation,
and PnP-removal helpers. It checks late USB identity metadata with unchanged
node names, container-only updates, query-failure cleanup, endpoint/media
registration removal, already-removed devices, foreign-class protection, and
removal failures. Fault injection verifies that failed property/PnP writes
do not publish new devices or lose pending arrival/rename state, and that
loading remembered endpoints does not claim another backend's registrations.
It does not require Wine or a PipeWire server. The PipeWire private-runtime
test separately checks real SetupAPI present-interface enumeration before
and after hotplug.

Notification-client lifetime:

    python3 notification-lifetime.py --build-dir /tmp/mmdevapi-notification-tests \
        --wine-build-dir /path/to/configured/wine/build

This fixture extracts the registration, unregistration and both dispatch paths.
It checks cancellation of saved snapshots, concurrent callback draining without
holding the registration lock, duplicate registrations, self-unregistration,
and shutdown with inaccessible callback records. Client IUnknown methods abort
if called. It does not establish Windows behavior for blocking callbacks or
cross-client unregistration inside callbacks; both violate the documented
IMMNotificationClient contract. The private PipeWire runtime suite tests real
endpoint/default events and rejects client AddRef/Release calls as well.
